e out cities and what had happened  to cities and there was this framework  uh very much a credit to tom segrow's  work that we had an urban crisis and we  were trying to figure out where we got  it  and how deep it was and for whom um and  i feel like we're now  at a level of specificity maybe now more  if we're actually investigating those  crises at a different level and then  part of it is just you know how we  identify ourselves as historians in  given moments maybe  i don't know if that to me that's what  it felt like but  briefly i think your your comment uh  makes me think of the recent article in  the jh by andrew sandoval strauss about  latino urbanisms and  um  and i do think it's interesting i mean i  think this is a moment  i think the earlier question of  globalization brought this up for me as  well  you know that the  well just the the the way in which the  uh  certainly what's happening during the  70s plays out differently in different  parts of the country and also people are  naming and labeling or trying to  understand a set of changes that we can  obviously see much more clearly in  retrospect but i think the idea of  globalization and the idea of  kind of how  cities might  come back from some of the problems that  the northeast and midwestern cities  might come back from some of the  problems that were especially pressing  at that time  we can see these things now in a way  that people at the time were trying to  understand make sense of  and interpret and  that the whole framework of  globalization or of a post-industrial  economy or of  gentrification and what that would mean  and different of new immigration flows  and how those would change  cities all of those are  are  just coming into existence and people  are grappling at the time to try to  understand those so i i guess that  that's one way of thinking about it and  i i also i might be asked your other  point i think that there is definitely a  strong  regional component to this and 